+# QMX network relay for a Synology NAS (Container Manager / Docker).
+# Talks to the QMX with libusb in userspace - no DSM kernel drivers needed.
+FROM python:3.12-alpine
+RUN apk add --no-cache libusb \
+ && pip install --no-cache-dir libusb1
+COPY qmx_nas_relay.py /app/qmx_nas_relay.py
+EXPOSE 7355
+ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-u", "/app/qmx_nas_relay.py"]
diff --git a/tools/nas-relay/README.md b/tools/nas-relay/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..8c6abf95
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/nas-relay/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# QMX on the Synology NAS → Tab5 over the network
+
+The QMX stays plugged into the Synology. A small relay runs **on the NAS
+itself** in a container, and the Tab5 connects to it directly over WiFi.
+No other computer is involved.
+
+```
+QMX ──USB──▶ Synology NAS  (qmx-relay container, libusb)
+                  │  TCP 7355 (QMXR/1: I/Q 48 kHz + CAT)
+                  ▼
+             Tab5 QMX-Panadapter   Radio → Radio source: NAS
+```
+
+The relay talks to the QMX in userspace through libusb, so it doesn't need
+any DSM kernel drivers (DSM doesn't ship USB audio drivers). It opens the QMX only
+while a Tab5 is connected, and releases it when the Tab5 disconnects.
+
+## 1. Install the relay on the NAS
+
+Container Manager (DSM 7.2+):
+
+1. Copy this folder (`Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `qmx_nas_relay.py`)
+   to the NAS, e.g. `/volume1/docker/qmx-relay`.
+2. Go to **Container Manager → Project → Create**. Set Path to that folder
+   and use the existing `docker-compose.yml`. Let it build, then start it.
+3. Check the container's **Log**. You should see
+   `listening on 0.0.0.0:7355 (QMXR/1)`.
+
+Or over SSH:
+
+```
+cd /volume1/docker/qmx-relay
+sudo docker compose up -d --build
+sudo docker logs -f qmx-relay
+```
+
+Check that the NAS sees the QMX:
+
+```
+sudo docker run --rm --privileged -v /dev/bus/usb:/dev/bus/usb qmx-nas-relay --probe
+```
+
+This lists the USB devices, marks the QMX, and shows its interfaces. You
+should see a CDC data interface with two bulk endpoints, and an audio
+interface `alt1` with an iso IN endpoint `0x83`.
+
+## 2. Point the Tab5 at the NAS
+
+On the Tab5's web page, open the **Radio** menu, click **Radio source**, and
+enter the NAS's IP address. It reads **Radio source: NAS (network)** once
+connected, and the panadapter, FT8 and CAT work exactly as they do over USB.
+Leave the field blank to go back to the QMX on the Tab5's own USB port.
+
+You can also set `relay_host` / `relay_port` in the Tab5 config file, or call
+`POST /api/relay {"host":"192.168.1.x","port":7355}`.
+`GET /api/relay` shows the link state, the I/Q sample count and the last error.
+
+## Behaviour
+
+* If the QMX is powered off or unplugged, the relay closes the connection
+  and the Tab5 treats it like a USB unplug. The Tab5 retries every ~2 s and
+  reconnects on its own when the QMX comes back.
+* The relay must be the only program on the NAS that uses the QMX. If the
+  log says `QMX is in use by another program`, stop whatever else holds it.
+* One Tab5 at a time. A new connection replaces the old one, so a rebooted
+  Tab5 doesn't get locked out by its own stale session.
+* **Radio menus** (the QMX's own menu system, on the web page's Radio menu)
+  work over the network too. Like on USB, they use the QMX's *second* serial
+  port, so CAT is never disturbed. Set **System config → GPS & Ser. ports →
+  USB serial ports = 2** on the radio (needs relay with terminal support, i.e.
+  this version). With one port, the Tab5 says so instead of opening.
+* While a NAS address is set, a QMX plugged into the Tab5's USB port is ignored.
+* If WiFi can't keep up, the relay drops I/Q frames rather than build up
+  delay. It never drops CAT. Drops are counted in the log.
+* Bandwidth is about 200 kB/s (48 kHz × 2 × 16-bit).
+
+## Bench test without the radio
+
+```
+python3 qmx_nas_relay.py --fake        # synthetic +3 kHz tone and a CAT emulator
+```
